---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Blk from "../components/Blk.astro";
import Note from "../components/Note.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import d from "../data/indexes.json";
const bad = d.rows.filter((r) => /nothing|fragment|only|unknown/.test(r.holds)).length;
---
<Base
  title="What the indexes actually hold"
  eyebrow={`${d.rows.length} holdings measured · ${d.checked}`}
  dek="Most of what this archive has failed to find, it failed to find in places that were never transcribed. This page is the measurement, kept separately because it is the difference between a null result and an empty search.">

  <section class="blk">
    <Note style="border-left-width:3px;margin-bottom:1.3rem">
      <strong>Read this first.</strong> {d.paywall}
      {" "}<a href={u("/berkeley-woman/")}>What it produced →</a>
    </Note>
    <p class="lede">{d.lede}</p>
    <p style="margin-top:1.1rem"><strong>The method is a control test.</strong> {d.method}</p>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1.1rem">Of the {d.rows.length} holdings measured here,
    <strong>{bad}</strong> turned out to be partial, empty or unreachable — and in every one of those
    cases this archive had already run a search against them and would have reported the emptiness as
    a fact.</p>
  </section>

  <Blk label="Measured, not assumed" title="Ten holdings">

    <div style="margin-top:1.3rem;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:1.4rem">
      {d.rows.map((r) => (
        <div style="border-left:2px solid var(--accent);padding:2px 0 2px 16px">
          <div>
            <span class="mono small" style="color:var(--ink-3)">{r.index}</span>
            <strong style="margin-left:.6rem">{r.place}</strong>
          </div>
          <p style="margin-top:.4rem"><strong>Holds: {r.holds}</strong></p>
          <p class="small" style="margin-top:.35rem;color:var(--ink-2)"><em>Test —</em> {r.test}</p>
          <p class="small" style="margin-top:.35rem">{r.means}{" "}
            <a href={u(r.href)}>Where it bites →</a></p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </Blk>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>The pattern, and why it matters more than any one entry</h2>
    <p class="lede">{d.lesson}</p>

    <Note>
      <strong>This is the rule the rest of the site is supposed to follow.</strong> Before reporting
      that something is not there, establish that the source covers the parish and the window. The
      two times this archive has broken that rule it produced published errors —
      <a href={u("/portsea/")}>a two-year transcript read as a searched parish</a>, and
      <a href={u("/bristol-chapels/")}>an untranscribed city read as an empty one</a> — and both are
      on <a href={u("/corrections/")}>the errors page</a> rather than quietly fixed.
    </Note>

  </section>

  <Blk label="This page, measured against itself" title="Twice the instrument was the thing at fault">
    <p class="lede" style="margin-top:1rem">A page that measures other people's indexes should be
    read the same way, and twice it has failed its own test. <strong>Both faults were in the
    instrument rather than in any source</strong>, and both would have been invisible to a reader.</p>

    <div style="margin-top:1.3rem;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:1.2rem">
      <div style="border-left:2px solid var(--accent);padding:2px 0 2px 16px">
        <div><span class="mono small" style="color:var(--ink-3)">15 September 2026</span>
          <strong style="margin-left:.6rem">A verdict displayed as something it did not mean</strong></div>
        <p class="small" style="margin-top:.35rem">The template that renders this table was a
        <strong>fallthrough chain</strong> — good, partial, absent, and everything else «cannot
        answer». So <strong>blocked</strong>, which means <em>a source nobody has asked, because it
        wants an account this archive will not make</em>, displayed as a source that had been asked
        and could not answer. Those are opposite statements: one is about this archive's choice and
        the other about the source's emptiness. One row also carried the freehand verdict «unusable
        as a control», which fell through the same way. <strong>The five words are now declared in
        the data, the template looks them up instead of falling through, and a gate refuses any
        verdict outside the list.</strong></p>
      </div>
      <div style="border-left:2px solid var(--accent);padding:2px 0 2px 16px">
        <div><span class="mono small" style="color:var(--ink-3)">27 September 2026</span>
          <strong style="margin-left:.6rem">A zero that belonged to the question, not the archive</strong></div>
        <p class="small" style="margin-top:.35rem">Trove's title endpoint returns its gazettes under
        the key <span class="mono">newspaper</span>. Asking it for a <span class="mono">gazette</span>
        key returns nothing, so the first run of the script that measures Australian holdings
        reported <strong>0 gazettes</strong> — and that was <strong>one commit from being published
        as a fact about Trove</strong>. It is a fact about the parser. The endpoint's own
        <span class="mono">total</span> is now compared with what the parser found and a disagreement
        refuses the build: newspaper 2,014 = total 2,014, gazette 38 = total 38.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <Note style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <strong>Both belong here rather than on the errors page alone</strong>, because a reader
      deciding how much to trust the verdicts above is entitled to know that the verdicts have twice
      been wrong for reasons that had nothing to do with the records.
      <a href={u("/corrections/")}>Every error, kept →</a>
    </Note>
  </Blk>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">

    <p class="small" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <a href={u("/searched/")}>Every source read, and what it gave →</a> ·
      <a href={u("/errands/")}>What can only be done in a record office →</a> ·
      <a href={u("/method/")}>How this archive works →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
